  "final_text": "All four steps are done. I checked the dashboard panel titles and the rule's enabled state by reading them back.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields. They include `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean) and `timestamp` (date). Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-926d7c9e Flights' (id `06b1f45d-725f-47e2-a5db-2e4f857a4154`) has exactly two panels. One is a line chart of flight count over time, and the other is a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-926d7c9e Cancelled flights' (id `273a74d7-35aa-4cb1-96f8-cbe549f36682`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents with `Cancelled: true` appear in the last 5 minutes. I created it with `enabled: true`, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards. It has no actions attached, so firing only records an alert and sends no notification.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** I changed the carrier panel title to 'Flights by carrier'. The dashboard now shows 'Flights over time' and 'Flights by carrier'. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
